WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:06.106
"Can Diet and Lifestyle Changes Boost Our GLP-1
Like Ozempic (Semaglutide) Does?"

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.375
I’ve talked about the upsides

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:11.078
of pharmacologically boosting
GLP-1 activity

00:00:11.078 --> 00:00:12.246
in terms of weight loss,

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:14.481
given the ravages of obesity,

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:16.416
and I’ve also discussed
its downsides,

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.752
including common
gastrointestinal reactions

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:20.053
and muscle loss,

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.989
as well as the rarer
and more speculative

00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:23.724
side effects
such as thyroid cancer,

00:00:23.724 --> 00:00:26.360
pancreatitis,
and pancreatic cancer.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.029
And I’ve summarized
the cost-benefit trade-off,

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:32.332
detailing for whom the benefits
may far outweigh the risks,

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:33.934
at least in the short term.

00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:36.370
But is there any way
to get the benefits of GLP-1

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:37.671
without the risks,

00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:41.141
by boosting it naturally
with diet and lifestyle changes?

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:43.977
Well, there’s exercise.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:46.480
It appears
to acutely increase GLP-1.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.916
There have been five studies
of exercise and GLP-1,

00:00:48.916 --> 00:00:50.284
and four out of five of them

00:00:50.284 --> 00:00:52.920
found higher concentrations
of total GLP-1

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.222
after High Intensity
Interval Training

00:00:55.222 --> 00:00:56.857
and Sprint Interval Training

00:00:56.857 --> 00:00:59.660
as well as Moderate Intensity
Continuous Training,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.229
with no apparent difference
between High Intensity Interval

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:04.097
and Moderate Intensity
Continuous.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.733
Compared to control groups

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.202
where the study subjects
didn’t exercise at all,

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:11.171
both types of physical activity

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:14.308
(High Intensity Interval and
Moderate Intensity Continuous)

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:17.411
induced significantly higher
GLP-1 concentrations

00:01:17.411 --> 00:01:19.179
immediately after exercise,

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:22.349
and 30-90 minutes later.

00:01:22.349 --> 00:01:25.285
Unfortunately, there appeared
to be publication bias,

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:28.455
meaning some trials
that failed to show an effect

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:30.023
may have been quietly shelved,

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.992
thereby skewing the results.

00:01:31.992 --> 00:01:34.928
But what’s the downside
of exercising?

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:36.596
The evidence is overwhelming

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.298
that nearly everyone
can benefit

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:40.567
from becoming
more physically active.

00:01:41.368 --> 00:01:43.603
What about diet?

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.472
There are a variety
of natural products,

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:46.840
foods, beverages, and spices,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.144
that have been shown to boost
our own natural GLP-1 levels.

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:53.146
But can endogenous GLP-1,

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.782
meaning GLP-1
from inside the body,

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.017
work as an alternative

00:01:57.017 --> 00:02:00.253
to the drugs that mimic GLP-1,
like Ozempic?

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.188
If you haven’t yet,

00:02:01.188 --> 00:02:03.357
I recommend watching
or re-watching my video

00:02:03.357 --> 00:02:05.826
about what GLP-1 is
and how it works,

00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:08.595
to best fully grasp
the issue at hand.

00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:11.331
Our natural GLP-1 levels
rise and fall

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:13.133
in response to meal ingestion.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.801
When we take
these drugs, though,

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:18.171
levels in our blood remain
relatively constant, and high.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:21.675
But "high" here
is a bit of an understatement.

00:02:21.675 --> 00:02:22.709
The total concentration

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:25.078
of these GLP-1 lookalike drugs
in our bloodstream

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:28.782
is typically around
20-30 nanomoles per liter.

00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:31.184
Okay, here’s how much GLP-1

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.053
rises naturally
in our bloodstream

00:02:33.053 --> 00:02:34.955
in the two hours
after we eat a meal,

00:02:34.955 --> 00:02:38.892
up to maybe 20 or 30 picomoles.

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:40.427
"Nano-" means billionth;

00:02:40.427 --> 00:02:42.796
"pico-" means trillionth.

00:02:42.796 --> 00:02:44.264
So, at this scale,

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:47.567
drug levels are up
to like 25,000.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.569
How could
boosting natural levels

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.638
even come close?

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:54.641
Well, part of the discrepancy
may be an artifact

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.877
of taking blood
from people’s veins

00:02:56.877 --> 00:02:58.111
to do blood tests.

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:00.981
Since natural GLP-1
is rapidly deactivated

00:03:00.981 --> 00:03:02.549
by an enzyme in our blood,

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:04.718
by the time GLP-1
is secreted by our gut,

00:03:04.718 --> 00:03:07.387
makes it through our liver,
heart, and lungs,

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:08.889
then out into our arteries,

00:03:08.889 --> 00:03:10.757
only to later reach the veins

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:12.959
where the lab tech
is pulling out blood,

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:16.196
the levels we see are lower
than the arterial levels

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.198
that GLP-1 receptors
are exposed to

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:19.533
throughout our body,

00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:22.069
though only
by a relatively small amount.

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:23.303
But how do drugs like Ozempic

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:26.173
sit around in the body
for weeks instead of minutes?

00:03:26.173 --> 00:03:27.507
They are chemically modified

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:31.344
to stick to the most abundant
protein in our blood.

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:34.181
So, even though the total
drug level in our blood

00:03:34.181 --> 00:03:36.016
is up in the nanomolar range,

00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:38.185
it’s so tightly bound to protein

00:03:38.185 --> 00:03:40.253
that the active level
in our blood

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:43.156
that’s free to actually interact
with tissue receptors

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:44.858
is like a hundred times smaller,

00:03:44.858 --> 00:03:47.761
at least down
in the picomolar range.

00:03:47.761 --> 00:03:49.362
Thus, although at first glance,

00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:50.831
it appears
that plasma drug levels

00:03:50.831 --> 00:03:52.799
are extremely high,

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:55.836
they actually may be closer
to those of natural GLP-1 levels

00:03:55.836 --> 00:03:57.804
after strong stimulation.

00:03:57.804 --> 00:04:01.341
But still, there seems
to be a huge discrepancy.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.479
It’s GLP-1 activation
in the brain

00:04:05.479 --> 00:04:08.515
that appears to account for
the regulation of food intake,

00:04:08.515 --> 00:04:10.050
but it isn’t currently clear

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.887
how this might be related
to GLP-1 in the blood.

00:04:13.887 --> 00:04:16.323
The primary source
of GLP-1 secretion

00:04:16.323 --> 00:04:18.125
in the body is the gut.

00:04:18.125 --> 00:04:21.595
So, wouldn’t it get eaten up
by enzymes in the blood

00:04:21.595 --> 00:04:24.431
before much of it
even made it into the brain?

00:04:24.431 --> 00:04:27.167
It can cross
the blood-brain barrier,

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.970
but is enough
really getting up there?

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.905
Well, GLP-1 is also produced

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:34.574
by nerves
in the brainstem itself,

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:35.742
and a large body of evidence

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:37.410
points to
the appetite-reducing effects

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.613
of GLP-1 mimicking drugs,
like Ozempic,

00:04:39.613 --> 00:04:43.383
being mediated by
these brain GLP-1 receptors.

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:45.886
So, how could
natural blood levels,

00:04:45.886 --> 00:04:48.221
expected after
dietary interventions,

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:50.690
aiming to increase
the secretion of gut GLP-1

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.094
still have
clinically relevant effects?

00:04:54.094 --> 00:04:56.797
Yes, the GLP-1 released
from our intestinal cells

00:04:56.797 --> 00:05:00.400
is rapidly degraded,
leading to low systemic levels,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.402
but its effects
appear to be mediated

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:05.672
by vagus nerve stimulation.

00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:08.375
The vagus nerve
connects the gut

00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:10.544
straight up
into the brainstem,

00:05:10.544 --> 00:05:12.712
so it’s directly wired
to the brain

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:15.816
and doesn’t have to rely
on blood transport.

00:05:15.816 --> 00:05:18.685
But wait, there are GLP-1
receptors on the pancreas,

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:20.353
the kidneys,
the liver, and the heart.

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:23.356
Why would the body
have GLP-1 receptors

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:25.025
on our peripheral organs,

00:05:25.025 --> 00:05:28.361
if the hormone acts mainly
through the vagus nerve?

00:05:28.361 --> 00:05:30.430
It’s possible
that strong stimuli

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.098
can boost levels
high enough in the blood

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:35.602
to activate
these peripheral receptors.

00:05:35.602 --> 00:05:38.171
So, perhaps,
the way GLP-1 normally works

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.606
is that we eat a meal,

00:05:39.606 --> 00:05:42.008
and GLP-1 sends
the appetite reducing signal

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:44.878
to our brain directly
through the vagus nerve,

00:05:44.878 --> 00:05:46.713
and then, our brain
relays the messages

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:49.516
to the relevant systems
throughout the body.

00:05:49.516 --> 00:05:51.384
Injecting people
with high levels

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:54.120
of a GLP-1-mimicking drug,
like Ozempic,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.957
means it can circulate
directly through the bloodstream

00:05:56.957 --> 00:05:58.959
to all these organs,
including the brain,

00:05:58.959 --> 00:06:01.928
and a strong
dietary GLP-1 stimulus

00:06:01.928 --> 00:06:03.964
might be able to do both.

00:06:03.964 --> 00:06:05.665
But you can’t really know

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:08.868
whether natural,
physiological levels of GLP-1

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:12.038
can suppress appetite,
until you...

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:14.441
put it to the test.

00:06:14.441 --> 00:06:15.875
When different levels of GLP-1

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.811
are dripped
straight into people’s veins,

00:06:17.811 --> 00:06:19.846
you can see
a dose-dependent reduction

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:22.549
in both hunger and food intake.

00:06:22.549 --> 00:06:25.785
Okay, but effective doses
used in infusion studies

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:27.954
quadrupled baseline levels
or more,

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:28.788
whereas the blood levels

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:31.524
increased by
GLP-1-boosting foods

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:33.760
tended to only double
baseline levels.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.062
So, while high-dose
GLP-1 infusion

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:39.099
drives a clear change
in hunger and eating behaviors,

00:06:39.099 --> 00:06:41.401
is it possible
that inducing GLP-1 secretion

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:43.503
through diet may not?

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:45.305
Well, remember how food

00:06:45.305 --> 00:06:48.975
naturally only boosts
blood levels up around 20?

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:51.077
It turns out you can get
a significant drop

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:52.112
in food intake

00:06:52.112 --> 00:06:55.548
up to 30-35% less
of an all-you-can-eat meal

00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:59.252
simply by getting your GLP-1
levels up to 10 or 15,

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.054
or even just 5!

00:07:01.054 --> 00:07:04.758
That is well within
doable dietary ranges.

00:07:04.758 --> 00:07:06.559
So, while low natural levels

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:09.296
might appear to have
little effect at first glance,

00:07:09.296 --> 00:07:12.599
a slight increase in levels
by dietary manipulation

00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:14.901
of GLP-1 secretion
could be sufficient

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:16.903
to have clinical relevance.

00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:18.071
Thus, we don’t have to strive

00:07:18.071 --> 00:07:21.441
for levels comparable to those
obtained using drugs.

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:24.177
And, of course,
this makes sense, right?

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:26.046
Why would our body
naturally produce

00:07:26.046 --> 00:07:28.815
an appetite-suppressing hormone
in response to food,

00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:31.885
if it didn’t end up
suppressing our appetite?

